From: =?UTF-8?q?Sergi=E3=83=BB=E3=82=BB=E3=83=AB=E3=82=B8?=
 <xerpi.g.12@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:49:33 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 39/52]  kernel: take callback waiters out of their sync object
 queues when running callbacks (#4289)

* ci: run the latest VitaAutotests

* kernel: keep whether a wait runs callbacks in its WaitTarget

Every wait passed whether it was the variant with callbacks next to its WaitTarget. The target now
carries it, and thread info adds the callback bit to the reported wait type from it. No behavior
change.

* kernel: keep an event flag's single waiter hold like a Vita

A flag without SCE_EVENT_WAITMULTIPLE refused every poll and wait while a waiter was queued, its
own thread's too. It now keeps a hold per wait of the thread that waits on it, like on a Vita: each
wait takes one, and a wake or a timeout gives it back. A wait that ends without blocking or taking
the bits keeps its hold until the thread ends or the flag is deleted. The holder's own polls and
waits pass.

* kernel: let waiting readers join readers that hold a rwlock

A rwlock handed nothing to its waiting readers while readers held it, so the readers queued behind
a writer that left the queue, like by its timeout, waited for every reader to let go. Like on a
Vita, every waiting reader now gets in while no writer holds or waits for the lock. The timeout
cases of head_leaves_more and timeout_serves_before_run get their read lock now.

* kernel: take a callback waiter out of its queue while its callbacks run

A thread that ran callbacks in a wait on a kernel object stayed in that object's queue, so the
object still counted it, could hand it what it waited for while the callback ran, and kept its
old place. Like on a Vita, the waiter now leaves the queue for its callbacks and the object serves
the waiters behind it. Once the callbacks ran, the waiter goes back in by its priority and gets
what it waits for at once if nobody ahead goes first, even past its deadline, and keeps the time
left from when it left for the callbacks. The notify itself doesn't take the waiter out yet, so it
leaves once its thread runs. The message pipe and the timer keep their own loops for now.

* kernel: keep an event flag's hold when its deadline passes during a callback

A single waiter event flag gave its hold back for every wait that ended by its timeout. On a Vita
only a queued wait that its deadline ends gives it back, while a wait whose deadline passed during
its callbacks ends without being queued again and keeps it, like one with a timeout of 0. The
wait now tells its object when a queued wait timed out, through a timed_out() step that the event
flag uses for its hold. event_flag_hold_callbacks passes.

* kernel: don't let a semaphore wait or poll take past queued waiters

A wait or poll the count covered took it even with a waiter queued ahead that needs more. Like on
a Vita, it now takes only when it would be first in the queue: never past a waiter in a FIFO queue,
and in a priority queue only past waiters of a lower priority. A waiter behind a head that leaves
by its timeout or for its callbacks gets its count then. sema_take_past_waiters passes now, and
poll_calls, sema_head_leaves and sema_requeue_wake match a Vita in their semaphore cases.

* kernel: report a woken thread as READY, and wait UID -1 without an object

Thread info showed a thread whose wait a waker had ended as still waiting until its host thread
went on, and wait UID 0 for a delay or a thread that waits on nothing. A waiting thread whose wait
is over now reports READY with no wait, as on a Vita, where the waker ends the wait, and a wait
without an object, like a delay, or no wait at all reports wait UID -1. delay_wait_id and
delay_variants pass.

(cherry picked from commit 96c9ed8910f0ca8bdbf71720940bc60b8f63064d)
